From 0f6d110925954d292b0ddccaeee6e17bc67d191e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John-Michael Mulesa <jmulesa@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 17:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: realtek: rtl826x: report the actual speed

rtl826x_read_status() takes the speed from the resolved advertisements.
When a 10GBASE-T link does not train at the highest common speed, the
PHY downshifts on its own (e.g. 10G -> 5G -> 2.5G) and the resolved
speed no longer matches the line: the link is reported as 10000 while
the PHY runs at 5000 or 2500.

Seen on a Hasivo S1100WP-8XGT-SE (RTL9303 + 2x RTL8264). The switch MAC
polls the PHY itself, so traffic kept flowing and the port LEDs showed
the real (lower) speed, but ethtool, sysfs and the "Link is Up" message
reported 10G, e.g. "resolved 10000, PHYSR 5000" and "resolved 5000,
PHYSR 2500".

Read the actual speed from PHYSR, as rtl822x_c45_read_status() already
does.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: John-Michael Mulesa <jmulesa@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/phy/realtek/realtek_main.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/net/phy/realtek/realtek_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/realtek/realtek_main.c
@@ -3574,6 +3574,19 @@ static int rtl826x_read_status(struct ph
 			return ret;
 	}
 
+	/* The PHY may downshift (e.g. from 10G to 5G or 2.5G) when the link
+	 * does not train at the highest common speed, so the resolved
+	 * speed can be wrong. Read the actual speed from the vendor register.
+	 */
+	if (phydev->link) {
+		ret = phy_read_mmd(phydev, MDIO_MMD_VEND2,
+				   RTL822X_VND2_C22_REG(RTL_PHYSR));
+		if (ret < 0)
+			return ret;
+
+		rtlgen_decode_physr(phydev, ret);
+	}
+
 	return genphy_c45_read_mdix(phydev);
 }
 
